No magic and no miracles,

265
00:09:30,067 --> 00:09:31,567
it's against the rules.

266
00:09:31,600 --> 00:09:35,200
If I go, I'll go...

267
00:09:35,233 --> 00:09:36,067
like this.

268
00:09:36,100 --> 00:09:39,867
-How dull.
-SERVO: Undercover angel.

269
00:09:39,900 --> 00:09:41,700
All right, is it a bet?

270
00:09:41,733 --> 00:09:43,033
MIKE: They're operating
under a different theology.

271
00:09:43,067 --> 00:09:45,767
Hmm, all right, it's a bet.

272
00:09:45,800 --> 00:09:47,900
I'll put this Bill Dudley
to the test.

273
00:09:49,033 --> 00:09:52,833
CROW: [ Imitating
Mission Impossible 's music ]

274
00:09:52,867 --> 00:09:55,267
Try new enriched stuff.

275
00:09:56,733 --> 00:09:59,300
MIKE: I just saw Howard Hughes
in the desert.

276
00:09:59,333 --> 00:10:01,367
Let me see if I got this
whole thing straight now.

277
00:10:01,400 --> 00:10:03,267
-You're a writer?
-Mm-hmm.

278
00:10:03,300 --> 00:10:04,433
And you wanna do an article

279
00:10:04,467 --> 00:10:06,200
on modern food merchandising.

280
00:10:06,233 --> 00:10:07,033
That's right.

281
00:10:07,067 --> 00:10:08,700
SERVO: And you work
for the devil.

282
00:10:08,733 --> 00:10:10,233
...okay for you to ride
with me today?

283
00:10:10,267 --> 00:10:11,533
CROWD: And it's the '50s.

284
00:10:11,567 --> 00:10:12,767
Well, it's okay with me, then.

285
00:10:12,800 --> 00:10:14,867
But I'd sure have a tough time
making my wife

286
00:10:14,900 --> 00:10:16,100
believe this one.

287
00:10:16,133 --> 00:10:17,567
Well, so would I.

288
00:10:17,600 --> 00:10:18,467
Come on.

289
00:10:18,500 --> 00:10:19,833
SERVO:
Well, you can bilocate

290
00:10:19,867 --> 00:10:21,233
and assume animal form.

291
00:10:21,267 --> 00:10:21,767
MIKE: Uh-huh.

292
00:10:21,800 --> 00:10:23,067
SERVO: Hmm.

293
00:10:23,100 --> 00:10:25,200
She's gonna blow the lid off the
whole bread wholesaling racket.

294
00:10:25,233 --> 00:10:26,433
She's naming names!

295
00:10:27,367 --> 00:10:28,767
CROW:
Ah, never mind those magazines,

296
00:10:28,800 --> 00:10:30,100
I barely read them.

297
00:10:30,133 --> 00:10:31,300
Except for American
Muscle Woman ,

298
00:10:31,333 --> 00:10:32,600
that's interesting.

299
00:10:34,567 --> 00:10:35,367
[ Music playing ]

300
00:10:35,400 --> 00:10:36,867
SERVO: Now we unload
the first van

301
00:10:36,900 --> 00:10:39,700
at William Conrad's house.

302
00:10:39,733 --> 00:10:42,000
MIKE: They leave orphaned
bread on people's doorsteps?

303
00:10:42,033 --> 00:10:43,567
[ Mimics baby crying ]

304
00:10:43,600 --> 00:10:46,733
[ Music playing ]

305
00:10:46,767 --> 00:10:49,367
MIKE: Why do they keep
the bread in dresser drawers?

306
00:10:49,400 --> 00:10:51,133
SERVO: This is Charles Kuralt.

307
00:10:51,167 --> 00:10:53,600
I'll leave you now
with images of bread.

308
00:10:54,633 --> 00:10:56,067
All right, my life is hell.

309
00:10:56,100 --> 00:10:57,733
What do you want from me?

310
00:10:57,767 --> 00:10:59,500
MIKE: How do you mess up
at this job?

311
00:11:00,967 --> 00:11:02,533
NARRATOR: Well,
that's how Whitey started out

312
00:11:02,567 --> 00:11:03,833
to prove her point.

313
00:11:03,867 --> 00:11:06,133
But Red was not far behind.

314
00:11:06,167 --> 00:11:07,933
MIKE: That's how Whitey
keeps us down.

315
00:11:07,967 --> 00:11:11,300
Hey, you're not down here
just for the ride, you know.

316
00:11:11,333 --> 00:11:13,833
Start working on the guy.
We got a bet on.

317
00:11:13,867 --> 00:11:15,900
CROW: Even the devil won't go
to The Bronx.

318
00:11:15,933 --> 00:11:19,067
[ Engine revving ]

319
00:11:19,100 --> 00:11:21,600
SERVO: It kind of sucks,
I got to take your notes, lady.

320
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:28,000
SERVO: Hey, can I split your top
and butter your buns?

321
00:11:28,033 --> 00:11:29,433
Hey, you know this seems to be

322
00:11:29,467 --> 00:11:32,067
an awfully dull job
that you have.

323
00:11:32,100 --> 00:11:34,467
Oh, what gave you that idea?

324
00:11:34,500 --> 00:11:35,867
Wasn't my--

325
00:11:35,900 --> 00:11:37,233
I mean,

326
00:11:37,267 --> 00:11:40,767
well, after all, it is just
a delivery man's job,

327
00:11:40,800 --> 00:11:42,567
-isn't it?
-CROW: Not just.

328
00:11:42,600 --> 00:11:43,733
Young lady, take it from me.

329
00:11:43,767 --> 00:11:45,633
Any guy who thinks
this is just a delivery job

330
00:11:45,667 --> 00:11:47,167
belongs in a coal truck

331
00:11:47,200 --> 00:11:48,300
not a bread route.

332
00:11:48,333 --> 00:11:50,000
SERVO: I'm running you in, lady.
You're under arrest

333
00:11:50,033 --> 00:11:51,800
for impeding bread delivery.

334
00:11:53,367 --> 00:11:54,667
MIKE: Well,
then Mabel said to me,

335
00:11:54,700 --> 00:11:56,000
"Well, why did you wear
those earrings?"

336
00:11:56,033 --> 00:11:58,233
And then we went to Nine West,
but we couldn't find anything

337
00:11:58,267 --> 00:11:59,767
we wanted,
because I have really wide feet.

338
00:11:59,800 --> 00:12:01,833
But sometimes,
I can find stuff at Payless.

339
00:12:01,867 --> 00:12:03,733
Anyhoo, Cindy told me
that Victoria's Secret

340
00:12:03,767 --> 00:12:04,900
was just around the corner,

341
00:12:04,933 --> 00:12:06,233
and she said
they're having a sale,

342
00:12:06,267 --> 00:12:07,833
and she knows
I'm really broke right now.

343
00:12:07,867 --> 00:12:10,333
So, I confronted her and well...

344
00:12:10,367 --> 00:12:12,367
SERVO: You know,
I better call the cops on her.

345
00:12:12,400 --> 00:12:14,267
CROW: I never sat on a
loaf of bread before,

346
00:12:14,300 --> 00:12:16,333
it feels great.

347
00:12:16,367 --> 00:12:18,967
SERVO: Maybe I should take that
job as a Mouseketeer.

348
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,100
[ Music playing ]

349
00:12:22,133 --> 00:12:23,633
CROW: If she doesn't shut up
pretty soon,

350
00:12:23,667 --> 00:12:25,767
I'm gonna stuff her
full of croutons.

351
00:12:29,167 --> 00:12:31,067
SERVO:
Get your buns out of my truck.

352
00:12:31,100 --> 00:12:32,633
CROW: Quit loafing in my van,

353
00:12:32,667 --> 00:12:34,300
you really take the cake.

354
00:12:34,333 --> 00:12:36,367
MIKE: You got a lot of crust
to come around here.

355
00:12:36,400 --> 00:12:37,900
[ Laughs ]

356
00:12:37,933 --> 00:12:40,900
SERVO: Come on, you and me,
mano a mano.

357
00:12:40,933 --> 00:12:42,667
Now, let's take a look
at the lighter side

358
00:12:42,700 --> 00:12:44,833
of wholesale bread delivery.

359
00:12:44,867 --> 00:12:46,200
Deet, dee, deet, boom.

360
00:12:46,233 --> 00:12:48,267
Young lady, you sit there,

361
00:12:48,300 --> 00:12:49,367
and I'll be right back.

362
00:12:49,400 --> 00:12:51,833
MIKE: I need some time alone
with my bread.

363
00:12:53,833 --> 00:12:55,400
Doesn't she look smart?

364
00:12:55,433 --> 00:12:58,967
Today's career gal
in her new fashions from...

365
00:13:00,400 --> 00:13:03,067
Hmm...

366
00:13:03,100 --> 00:13:05,533
The Mahavishnu Orchestra.

367
00:13:05,567 --> 00:13:06,900
Well?

368
00:13:07,667 --> 00:13:08,700
Well,

369
00:13:09,567 --> 00:13:11,267
I told him I thought
he was a dope.

370
00:13:11,300 --> 00:13:13,967
Not to work, but to be the
first one in, every day.

371
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:15,467
-RED: Good.
-CROW: Stanley.

372
00:13:15,500 --> 00:13:19,100
I told him I thought all this
push on getting good displays,

373
00:13:19,133 --> 00:13:20,967
just a lot of front office talk.

374
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,300
Couldn't have phrased it
better myself.

375
00:13:23,333 --> 00:13:25,467
"Why keep a close check on
how your load's going?"

376
00:13:25,500 --> 00:13:26,633
I said.

377
00:13:26,667 --> 00:13:30,200
"You can always dump it
on the last half dozen stops."

378
00:13:30,233 --> 00:13:32,400
Excellent.
And what did he say?

379
00:13:32,433 --> 00:13:33,700
Nothing.

380
00:13:33,733 --> 00:13:34,500
That's the trouble.

381
00:13:34,533 --> 00:13:35,733
He just sat there listening

382
00:13:35,767 --> 00:13:38,400
and looking very thoughtful.

383
00:13:38,433 --> 00:13:39,767
There, you see?

384
00:13:39,800 --> 00:13:40,867
What did I tell you?

385
00:13:40,900 --> 00:13:43,267
My bet's practically won.

386
00:13:43,300 --> 00:13:45,767
I would've sworn that nothing
could make him

387
00:13:45,800 --> 00:13:48,700
slip back into his
old selling ways.

388
00:13:48,733 --> 00:13:49,833
Uh-oh.

389
00:13:50,833 --> 00:13:52,667
[ Mike mimics ]

390
00:13:52,700 --> 00:13:53,667
BILL: Hmm.

391
00:13:53,700 --> 00:13:55,333
Must be new on the force.

392
00:13:55,367 --> 00:13:57,767
MIKE: I got donuts.
Come back.

393
00:13:57,800 --> 00:14:00,433
You talked, and I've listened.

394
00:14:00,467 --> 00:14:02,267
Now it's my turn.

395
00:14:02,300 --> 00:14:04,267
I talk and you listen.

396
00:14:04,300 --> 00:14:06,067
First of all, take a look.

397
00:14:06,933 --> 00:14:07,867
A loaf of bread.

398
00:14:07,900 --> 00:14:09,300
CROW: Eat every piece.

399
00:14:09,333 --> 00:14:10,600
BILL: An important food,

400
00:14:10,633 --> 00:14:11,933
a healthy food.

401
00:14:11,967 --> 00:14:13,633
And a basic food
that can be served

402
00:14:13,667 --> 00:14:15,400
in more than a hundred
different ways.

403
00:14:15,433 --> 00:14:17,133
-SERVO: Actually, one.
-BILL: And give a look.

404
00:14:17,167 --> 00:14:19,000
Not just one kind of bread.

405
00:14:19,033 --> 00:14:20,567
But a whole variety.

406
00:14:20,600 --> 00:14:21,900
And not only bread.

407
00:14:21,933 --> 00:14:24,800
But a whole variety
of all types of baked goods.

408
00:14:24,833 --> 00:14:25,600
SERVO: Oh.

409
00:14:25,633 --> 00:14:26,933
Are you, by any chance,

410
00:14:26,967 --> 00:14:28,733
trying to say that you think
this business

411
00:14:28,767 --> 00:14:30,133
of yours is important?

412
00:14:30,167 --> 00:14:31,900
That's exactly the way
I feel about it.

413
00:14:31,933 --> 00:14:34,467
MIKE: I have to, it's all I got.

414
00:14:34,500 --> 00:14:36,700
There's something
about that guy's tone of voice

415
00:14:36,733 --> 00:14:37,867
I don't like.

416
00:14:37,900 --> 00:14:39,233
-CROW: It's so butch.
-But just because

417
00:14:39,267 --> 00:14:41,267
we're important in the food
picture, doesn't mean a guy

418
00:14:41,300 --> 00:14:43,933
can sit back
and take it easy on this job.

419
00:14:43,967 --> 00:14:44,733
Why not?

420
00:14:44,767 --> 00:14:47,767
[ Music playing ]

421
00:14:51,400 --> 00:14:55,733
Huh, it's jerks like that
that end up as supervisors.

422
00:14:55,767 --> 00:14:58,167
[ Music playing ]

423
00:14:58,200 --> 00:15:00,533
CROW:
It's the circus of the Devil.

424
00:15:02,167 --> 00:15:04,767
SERVO: No, thanks,
I use toilet paper.

425
00:15:04,800 --> 00:15:06,533
MIKE: If you're gonna
tempt a guy, don't send

426
00:15:06,567 --> 00:15:07,700
Clare Boothe Luce.

427
00:15:08,467 --> 00:15:09,500
SERVO: Please observe silence

428
00:15:09,533 --> 00:15:11,000
while visiting the
bread memorial.

429
00:15:14,167 --> 00:15:14,833
BILL: You know...

430
00:15:14,867 --> 00:15:15,867
MIKE: Sausage.

431
00:15:15,900 --> 00:15:17,633
BILL: ...when I first started
on the route,

432
00:15:17,667 --> 00:15:20,067
I acted like it was nothing more
than a delivery job.

433
00:15:20,100 --> 00:15:21,067
CROW: I don't care.

434
00:15:21,100 --> 00:15:22,567
Take this grocer
goodwill business.

435
00:15:22,600 --> 00:15:24,433
CROW:
There's not an article in this.

436
00:15:25,600 --> 00:15:28,367
Why, I used to go into stores,
and slam doors.

437
00:15:28,400 --> 00:15:29,400
MIKE: Hey, Dr. Giggles.

438
00:15:29,433 --> 00:15:31,267
BILL: Hiya, Duffy old boy.

439
00:15:31,300 --> 00:15:33,167
How's the old miser today?

440
00:15:33,200 --> 00:15:34,733
Counting your gold again, huh?

441
00:15:34,767 --> 00:15:37,067
SERVO: I had a Brooklyn accent,
and a different personality.

442
00:15:37,100 --> 00:15:39,433
[ Bill whistles ]

443
00:15:39,467 --> 00:15:40,700
CROW: When bread wholesalers

444
00:15:40,733 --> 00:15:42,467
terrorize the community.

445
00:15:43,767 --> 00:15:45,733
MIKE: Everybody down,
the bread man is here.

446
00:15:45,767 --> 00:15:47,233
SERVO: I'll do anything you say.

447
00:15:47,267 --> 00:15:48,833
CROW:
Let the reign of terror end.

448
00:15:48,867 --> 00:15:50,000
SERVO: Oh.

449
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:54,933
MIKE:
That's Christine Jorgensen.

450
00:15:56,600 --> 00:15:58,233
Oh, sorry, Duff.

451
00:16:00,567 --> 00:16:02,100
CROW:
Not the Happy's ripple chips.

452
00:16:02,133 --> 00:16:03,267
So I'm done, huh?

453
00:16:05,100 --> 00:16:06,400
See you again, Duff.

454
00:16:07,267 --> 00:16:08,100
[ Laughs ]

455
00:16:08,133 --> 00:16:09,233
It must've been awful.

456
00:16:09,267 --> 00:16:11,333
Well, maybe it wasn't quite
that bad.

457
00:16:11,367 --> 00:16:13,433
Anyway, today,
I go after a grocer's goodwill,

458
00:16:13,467 --> 00:16:16,333
a little different...
I'll show you.

459
00:16:16,367 --> 00:16:18,200
SERVO: Watch me come on
to a grocer.

460
00:16:21,967 --> 00:16:23,500
-BILL: Morning, Mr. Marco.
-Good morning.

461
00:16:23,533 --> 00:16:26,267
CROW: Ah, just putting
the salmon balls away.

462
00:16:26,300 --> 00:16:28,567
MIKE: Mr. Marco,
you want my coffee ring today?

463
00:16:28,600 --> 00:16:30,100
Sure be a shame
if something bad happened

464
00:16:30,133 --> 00:16:31,700
to your store here.

465
00:16:31,733 --> 00:16:33,533
How are you today?

466
00:16:33,567 --> 00:16:34,933
I'm feeling lousy.

467
00:16:34,967 --> 00:16:36,167
Oh, I'm sorry to hear that.

468
00:16:36,200 --> 00:16:37,500
SERVO:
Because I like you, Mr. Marco.

469
00:16:37,533 --> 00:16:39,067
Hey, something new.

470
00:16:39,100 --> 00:16:41,167
MIKE: It'd be a shame
if this ran over your kid.

471
00:16:41,200 --> 00:16:43,667
Hey, these are pretty slick.

472
00:16:43,700 --> 00:16:44,933
You noticed that, eh?

473
00:16:44,967 --> 00:16:46,133
Sure did.

474
00:16:46,167 --> 00:16:48,967
Now, I bet your customers
will go for them, too.

475
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:52,133
With my customers,
maybe yes, maybe no.

476
00:16:52,167 --> 00:16:53,800
-We'll see.
-Go on, you know doggone

477
00:16:53,833 --> 00:16:55,300
-well they will.
-CROW: Okay, they will,

478
00:16:55,333 --> 00:16:57,300
-but don't hurt me.
-Be right back.

479
00:16:58,200 --> 00:16:59,833
SERVO: I played it nice
this time.

480
00:16:59,867 --> 00:17:01,300
-See?
-You see?

481
00:17:01,333 --> 00:17:03,300
I want every grocer
on my route thinking...

482
00:17:03,333 --> 00:17:04,367
MIKE: What a moron.

483
00:17:04,400 --> 00:17:07,267
MARCO: That Bill Dudley is okay.

484
00:17:07,300 --> 00:17:09,633
Never slams doors.

485
00:17:09,667 --> 00:17:12,100
Always seems friendly,
always got a smile.

486
00:17:12,133 --> 00:17:13,133
SERVO: What's he on?

487
00:17:13,167 --> 00:17:14,700
MAN: Always interested
in my store,

488
00:17:14,733 --> 00:17:16,467
treats my place with respect.

489
00:17:16,500 --> 00:17:17,800
CROW: What does he want from me?

490
00:17:17,833 --> 00:17:19,233
MAN: Never slams
his trays around,

491
00:17:19,267 --> 00:17:20,600
or makes a nuisance
of himself.

492
00:17:20,633 --> 00:17:21,933
SERVO:
Why can't he leave me alone?

493
00:17:21,967 --> 00:17:23,967
MAN: I like to do business
with salesman like that.

494
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,167
MIKE: But his bread sucks.

495
00:17:26,200 --> 00:17:28,700
BILL: Believe me, building
up goodwill is important.

496
00:17:28,733 --> 00:17:30,633
Without it, you get nowhere
in selling a day.

497
00:17:30,667 --> 00:17:32,533
Look, why don't you stay here
and rest a minute

498
00:17:32,567 --> 00:17:34,067
while I go in
and check the sales?

499
00:17:34,100 --> 00:17:36,067
-Don't run off now.
-Oh, I wouldn't think of it.

500
00:17:36,100 --> 00:17:39,033
CROW: Devil's gonna show up
as a hooker now.

501
00:17:39,067 --> 00:17:40,600
[ Music playing ]

502
00:17:40,633 --> 00:17:42,267
The Mahavishnu Orchestra.

503
00:17:44,267 --> 00:17:46,167
I don't like the way
that guy's yakking.

504
00:17:46,200 --> 00:17:47,233
Well, I do.

505
00:17:47,267 --> 00:17:48,733
-You would.
-MIKE: You're a girl.

506
00:17:48,767 --> 00:17:50,667
You want my opinion?
He's in a rut.

507
00:17:50,700 --> 00:17:52,400
Oh, well, if you want mine,

508
00:17:52,433 --> 00:17:54,433
he's in the groove.

509
00:17:54,467 --> 00:17:56,633
Huh, thanks for the match, lady.

510
00:17:56,667 --> 00:17:57,867
What match? Oh.

511
00:17:57,900 --> 00:17:59,000
Oh, yes.
You're welcome.

512
00:17:59,033 --> 00:18:00,900
Haven't I seen that guy
before somewhere?

513
00:18:00,933 --> 00:18:02,867
Beer wine, wine made
from fresh beer.

514
00:18:02,900 --> 00:18:05,300
Now, I was gonna tell you about
the second bull's-eye of mine.

515
00:18:05,333 --> 00:18:06,433
Plus volume.

516
00:18:06,467 --> 00:18:09,133
One way to go after
it if you use this.

517
00:18:09,167 --> 00:18:11,600
-And this.
-CROW: A hat and a pad.

518
00:18:11,633 --> 00:18:13,933
That, my young lady,
is my business bible.

519
00:18:13,967 --> 00:18:15,200
SERVO: Ah.

520
00:18:15,233 --> 00:18:17,267
BILL: I've got a complete
and up-to-date picture

521
00:18:17,300 --> 00:18:19,233
on every stop on my route.

522
00:18:19,267 --> 00:18:20,767
SERVO: Measurements
of every woman in my routes.

523
00:18:20,800 --> 00:18:22,600
BILL: I know exactly
what sells in each store,

524
00:18:22,633 --> 00:18:23,700
and what doesn't.

525
00:18:23,733 --> 00:18:26,033
I know what new items
I've tried in each stop,

526
00:18:26,067 --> 00:18:27,500
how each one is done.

527
00:18:27,533 --> 00:18:28,767
No guesswork.

528
00:18:28,800 --> 00:18:31,300
The facts are all there,
and they're up-to-date.

529
00:18:31,333 --> 00:18:32,467
MIKE: You're reaching out,
aren't you?

530
00:18:32,500 --> 00:18:34,000
Well, team up these facts

531
00:18:34,033 --> 00:18:35,533
with a little bit of this,

532
00:18:35,567 --> 00:18:37,433
and you can go after plus volume
without the risk

533
00:18:37,467 --> 00:18:39,200
of getting stuck
with a lot of returns.

534
00:18:39,233 --> 00:18:41,467
So a guy gets returns.

535
00:18:41,500 --> 00:18:43,033
So what?

536
00:18:43,067 --> 00:18:45,433
Let the boss worry about him.

537
00:18:45,467 --> 00:18:46,333
Huh.

538
00:18:46,367 --> 00:18:48,400
SERVO:
Going over to Al Lewis' house.

539
00:18:49,533 --> 00:18:51,800
CROW: But why does
the strip club need bread?

540
00:18:51,833 --> 00:18:54,267
Here's something else a fella
can do to get plus volume.

541
00:18:54,300 --> 00:18:55,533
Watch me in here.

542
00:18:57,600 --> 00:18:58,700
[ Car honks ]

543
00:18:59,500 --> 00:19:00,800
MIKE: Oh, no, run.

544
00:19:00,833 --> 00:19:02,433
I can't believe I had to
waste him.

545
00:19:02,467 --> 00:19:04,667
CROW: You didn't have
to shoot him the face.

546
00:19:06,133 --> 00:19:07,567
MIKE: Oops, forgot the bread.

547
00:19:07,600 --> 00:19:08,833
[ Laughs ]

548
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:12,900
BILL: Say, Mr. O'Connor.

549
00:19:12,933 --> 00:19:14,833
MIKE: Oh, I don't live
with my mother.

550
00:19:14,867 --> 00:19:16,600
BILL:
I'm putting in a half a dozen

551
00:19:16,633 --> 00:19:18,300
of these new sweet doughs.

552
00:19:18,333 --> 00:19:19,200
I noticed they were good
last year,

553
00:19:19,233 --> 00:19:20,200
we're running them again.

554
00:19:20,233 --> 00:19:22,500
CROW: You're telling me
how to run my store?

555
00:19:22,533 --> 00:19:23,833
...I'm sure you wanna keep an
eye on them

556
00:19:23,867 --> 00:19:25,367
and see how they're going.

557
00:19:25,400 --> 00:19:27,267
SERVO: Look, I got a restraining
order on you.

558
00:19:27,300 --> 00:19:28,800
See for yourself
how they good they are.

559
00:19:28,833 --> 00:19:30,367
Well, thanks, Bill.

560
00:19:30,400 --> 00:19:31,333
SERVO: [ Munching noises ]

561
00:19:31,367 --> 00:19:33,367
MIKE: Take it out
of the wrapper first.

562
00:19:33,400 --> 00:19:35,233
SERVO: [ Gulping ] [ Burps ]

563
00:19:35,267 --> 00:19:36,200
You see?

564
00:19:36,233 --> 00:19:37,133
Talking it up

565
00:19:37,167 --> 00:19:39,233
is another key
to getting plus volume.

566
00:19:39,267 --> 00:19:42,033
You can get in a good plug
when you introduce

567
00:19:42,067 --> 00:19:43,467
a new package,

568
00:19:43,500 --> 00:19:45,267
a new product, or a special.

569
00:19:45,300 --> 00:19:46,567
CROW: Yeah, that's how it works.

570
00:19:46,600 --> 00:19:47,767
The more you talk it up,

571
00:19:47,800 --> 00:19:49,467
the better chance
of getting him to push it.

572
00:19:49,500 --> 00:19:50,800
SERVO: And the kids will buy it.

573
00:19:50,833 --> 00:19:52,733
But what was all this mumbo-
jumbo business

574
00:19:52,767 --> 00:19:54,433
with so many of this,
at so much,

575
00:19:54,467 --> 00:19:56,433
and so many of that,
at so much.

576
00:19:56,467 --> 00:19:57,400
It's a nice store.

577
00:19:57,433 --> 00:19:59,267
Oh, the count check.

578
00:19:59,967 --> 00:20:01,433
That's something I always do

579
00:20:01,467 --> 00:20:03,367
before I put the stuff
on the shelves.

580
00:20:03,400 --> 00:20:04,933
Avoids mix-ups later on.

581
00:20:04,967 --> 00:20:06,833
-MIKE: Thanks for the carousel.
-BILL: And this is especially

582
00:20:06,867 --> 00:20:08,900
important in big bakery
departments.

583
00:20:08,933 --> 00:20:10,667
You see, in supermarkets,

584
00:20:10,700 --> 00:20:13,000
there are a lot of bakery
salesmen coming in.

585
00:20:13,033 --> 00:20:14,533
-CROW: They're like roaches.
-BILL: The count check

586
00:20:14,567 --> 00:20:16,567
also reminds the grocer
of what baked goods

587
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:17,867
of mine he's got to sell.

588
00:20:17,900 --> 00:20:19,200
SERVO: Niels Bohr
buys groceries.

589
00:20:19,233 --> 00:20:20,567
BILL: The better he knows
my products,

590
00:20:20,600 --> 00:20:21,900
the better chance
of getting his help

591
00:20:21,933 --> 00:20:23,100
in selling them.

592
00:20:23,133 --> 00:20:25,167
And that makes
for plus volume for me.

593
00:20:25,200 --> 00:20:26,167
MIKE: That means money.

594
00:20:26,200 --> 00:20:28,433
Money I can cover
my naked butt with.

595
00:20:28,467 --> 00:20:29,800
Third bull's-eye of mine.

596
00:20:29,833 --> 00:20:32,133
Selling displays.
You know...

597
00:20:32,167 --> 00:20:33,600
CROW: I'm filled
with resentment.

598
00:20:33,633 --> 00:20:35,133
...that can be a little rough.

599
00:20:35,167 --> 00:20:36,667
You see,

600
00:20:36,700 --> 00:20:39,300
well, take this route of mine.

601
00:20:39,333 --> 00:20:41,100
I've got all kinds
of bread racks

602
00:20:41,133 --> 00:20:42,567
and display shelves.

603
00:20:42,600 --> 00:20:44,467
Some of them are pips.

604
00:20:44,500 --> 00:20:47,400
I guess other guys' routes
are the same way.

605
00:20:47,433 --> 00:20:49,700
I used to give up easy
on displays.

606
00:20:49,733 --> 00:20:51,367
I had only one rule I followed.

607
00:20:51,400 --> 00:20:52,567
SERVO: Kill or be killed.

608
00:20:52,600 --> 00:20:54,633
BILL: As long as the stuff
didn't fall on the floor

609
00:20:54,667 --> 00:20:56,700
while I was there, it was okay.

610
00:20:56,733 --> 00:20:58,700
-MIKE: I was a sick man.
-BILL: No more, though.

611
00:20:58,733 --> 00:21:00,467
SERVO: Now, I just throw it
on the floor.

612
00:21:00,500 --> 00:21:02,267
And believe me, there's
no trick or mystery to building

613
00:21:02,300 --> 00:21:03,167
good-selling displays.

614
00:21:03,200 --> 00:21:05,900
SERVO: Well, a platypus
could do my job.

615
00:21:05,933 --> 00:21:08,967
Just stack the bread
so the brand name shows best.

616
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
And shows off the bread
to best advantage.

617
00:21:11,033 --> 00:21:12,333
MIKE: I love each of my loaves.

618
00:21:12,367 --> 00:21:15,667
And give the specialty loaves
the best display space.

619
00:21:15,700 --> 00:21:17,800
And don't squash bread
to get it in.

620
00:21:17,833 --> 00:21:20,033
If a store is selling more
bread that I got room for

621
00:21:20,067 --> 00:21:22,467
-in my display space...
-CROW: Then we got a problem.

622
00:21:22,500 --> 00:21:24,833
BILL: ...I talk to the grocer
about giving me more room.

623
00:21:24,867 --> 00:21:27,000
Say, near the checkout counters,

624
00:21:27,033 --> 00:21:29,000
or on the counter
next to the register.

625
00:21:29,033 --> 00:21:31,100
SERVO: Maybe I rough him up,
maybe I bust his chops.

626
00:21:31,133 --> 00:21:33,733
Now, baked goods
are impulse items, too.

627
00:21:33,767 --> 00:21:36,733
So I always display them
as near eye-level as I can

628
00:21:36,767 --> 00:21:37,933
so they're easy to see...

629
00:21:37,967 --> 00:21:39,900
-ROM: Pffft.
-...and easy to reach.

630
00:21:39,933 --> 00:21:41,867
MIKE: Never put donuts
next to the kitty litter.

631
00:21:41,900 --> 00:21:43,500
BILL: When you figure

632
00:21:43,533 --> 00:21:44,967
a shopper spends an average

633
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:46,900
of only 35 seconds

634
00:21:46,933 --> 00:21:48,067
in a baked goods department...

635
00:21:48,100 --> 00:21:49,933
SERVO: Psst, hey, lady.
Eat me.

636
00:21:49,967 --> 00:21:51,300
BILL: ...for your display
to work on them.

637
00:21:51,333 --> 00:21:52,633
SERVO: I'm a specialty bread.
Eat me.

638
00:21:52,667 --> 00:21:55,300
BILL: So display it
within easy reach,

639
00:21:55,333 --> 00:21:57,200
group the items together,

640
00:21:57,233 --> 00:21:59,233
keep from burying any item
out of sight.

641
00:21:59,267 --> 00:22:00,733
CROW: In a shallow grave.

642
00:22:01,433 --> 00:22:02,467
But, you know...

643
00:22:02,500 --> 00:22:04,333
MIKE: Sometimes, I wanna put
a bullet in my skull.

644
00:22:04,367 --> 00:22:06,233
A lot of bread boys
are doing that much,

645
00:22:06,267 --> 00:22:07,933
so how do you get
the jump on them?

646
00:22:07,967 --> 00:22:09,267
SERVO:
Maybe rough them up a little.

647
00:22:09,300 --> 00:22:11,767
With special display
and promotion ideas.

648
00:22:11,800 --> 00:22:12,733
Take the other day.

649
00:22:13,933 --> 00:22:16,200
I noticed a stop on my route
was running a special

650
00:22:16,233 --> 00:22:17,567
on hamburger.

651
00:22:17,600 --> 00:22:18,800
Okay.

652
00:22:18,833 --> 00:22:21,133
So, I talked to the grocer
into including rolls

653
00:22:21,167 --> 00:22:22,867
in a special display
next to the hamburgers.

654
00:22:22,900 --> 00:22:23,933
CROW: It didn't work.

655
00:22:23,967 --> 00:22:26,200
BILL: That's where getting
goodwill pays off.

656
00:22:26,233 --> 00:22:28,133
If you got
the grocer's goodwill,

657
00:22:28,167 --> 00:22:30,533
you can get extra
selling displays in the store

658
00:22:30,567 --> 00:22:32,100
built around special
promotions.

659
00:22:32,133 --> 00:22:33,067
SERVO: Mm-hmm.

660
00:22:33,100 --> 00:22:34,500
-BILL: Picnic displays.
-MIKE: Oh, lovely.

661
00:22:34,533 --> 00:22:36,500
BILL: Especially good
just before holidays

662
00:22:36,533 --> 00:22:38,533
and weekends, and summer.

663
00:22:38,567 --> 00:22:40,467
School lunch idea displays,

664
00:22:40,500 --> 00:22:41,833
especially good in fall.

665
00:22:41,867 --> 00:22:43,567
MIKE: He's going over the edge,
stop him.

666
00:22:43,600 --> 00:22:45,133
BILL: Better breakfast displays.

667
00:22:45,167 --> 00:22:47,633
Or any others that tie
with national promotions.

668
00:22:47,667 --> 00:22:49,167
SERVO: I'm making
over three million a year.

669
00:22:49,200 --> 00:22:51,100
BILL:
Seasonal promotion displays.

670
00:22:51,133 --> 00:22:54,033
Fruitcakes around Thanksgiving
and Christmas.

671
00:22:54,067 --> 00:22:56,500
Hot cross buns and
Easter cakes at Easter time.

672
00:22:56,533 --> 00:22:57,900
MIKE: Funeral breads,
war breads.

673
00:22:57,933 --> 00:22:59,633
BILL:
Displays that suggest ideas

674
00:22:59,667 --> 00:23:01,533
for all types of entertaining.

675
00:23:01,567 --> 00:23:03,133
SERVO: Okay, we'll buy
the bread!

676
00:23:03,167 --> 00:23:04,833
BILL: Now you go with
the banners, and cards,

677
00:23:04,867 --> 00:23:08,333
and streamers, or printed
promotional material I can get.

678
00:23:08,367 --> 00:23:11,267
Anything to give any display
more sell.

679
00:23:11,300 --> 00:23:12,767
CROW: Triumph of the bread.

680
00:23:12,800 --> 00:23:15,767
But all this talk of mine
boils down to just this.

681
00:23:15,800 --> 00:23:17,067
This is a tough business,

682
00:23:17,100 --> 00:23:18,400
a mighty competitive business.

683
00:23:18,433 --> 00:23:19,467
MIKE: I've been shot twice.

684
00:23:19,500 --> 00:23:21,000
But the guy who aims,

685
00:23:21,033 --> 00:23:22,667
for grocer goodwill,

686
00:23:22,700 --> 00:23:25,033
plus volume,
and selling displays...

687
00:23:25,067 --> 00:23:26,067
SERVO: Divided by four.

688
00:23:26,100 --> 00:23:27,600
...they can be a darn
good one, too.

689
00:23:27,633 --> 00:23:30,167
Well, now,
that was quite a speech.

690
00:23:30,200 --> 00:23:32,333
Oh, I'm sorry
if I bored you, I...

691
00:23:32,367 --> 00:23:33,533
Bored me?
Oh, no, no.

692
00:23:33,567 --> 00:23:34,600
Quite the opposite.

693
00:23:34,633 --> 00:23:36,667
-MIKE: You terrified me.
-Tell me, how in the world

694
00:23:36,700 --> 00:23:38,700
do you get
all those things done?

695
00:23:38,733 --> 00:23:41,100
Oh, the whole secret to that

696
00:23:41,133 --> 00:23:42,733
is taking it in small chunks.

697
00:23:42,767 --> 00:23:44,100
CROW: And uppers, dexies,
speed...

698
00:23:44,133 --> 00:23:45,433
BILL: Maybe one week
I'm shooting

699
00:23:45,467 --> 00:23:46,600
for special displays

700
00:23:46,633 --> 00:23:48,467
in ten of my best stops.

701
00:23:48,500 --> 00:23:51,233
Next week, ten more, and so on.

702
00:23:51,267 --> 00:23:54,000
After all,
that's only two a day.

703
00:23:54,033 --> 00:23:56,167
Or maybe my goal
for this week is to spend

704
00:23:56,200 --> 00:23:57,600
a couple of extra minutes

705
00:23:57,633 --> 00:23:59,167
in my biggest volume stops

706
00:23:59,200 --> 00:24:00,833
just to work on goodwill.

707
00:24:00,867 --> 00:24:02,567
SERVO: I steal bikes
on the side.

708
00:24:02,600 --> 00:24:04,433
I always got something cooking

709
00:24:04,467 --> 00:24:05,100
at a few stops.

710
00:24:06,233 --> 00:24:08,833
Always something aimed
at one of these bull's-eyes,

711
00:24:08,867 --> 00:24:11,433
grocer goodwill,
plus volume,

712
00:24:11,467 --> 00:24:12,800
selling displays.

713
00:24:12,833 --> 00:24:14,367
And are you sure nobody

714
00:24:14,400 --> 00:24:16,167
could unsell you
on these things?

715
00:24:16,200 --> 00:24:17,667
Not a chance.

716
00:24:17,700 --> 00:24:19,033
I like the payoff too much.

717
00:24:19,067 --> 00:24:21,633
-MIKE: It's five bucks a week.
-Well, don't you ever slip up?

718
00:24:21,667 --> 00:24:22,800
CROW: You coming on to me?

719
00:24:22,833 --> 00:24:25,367
Sure, I'm just as human
as the next guy.

720
00:24:25,400 --> 00:24:27,500
That's why I keep these
bull's-eyes handy

721
00:24:27,533 --> 00:24:29,433
so that a small slip up

722
00:24:29,467 --> 00:24:31,567
doesn't turn
into a big trip up.

723
00:24:31,600 --> 00:24:32,367
SERVO: Oh.
[ Laughs ]

724
00:24:32,400 --> 00:24:33,467
Well, off to the races.

725
00:24:33,500 --> 00:24:34,433
What do you say?

726
00:24:34,467 --> 00:24:36,267
MIKE: Make sure
you stock your truck up

727
00:24:36,300 --> 00:24:39,200
so that you don't--
well, you know.

728
00:24:39,233 --> 00:24:41,467
CROW: Damn you all,
to my place.

729
00:24:42,767 --> 00:24:45,667
MIKE: Oh, it's gonna turn out
the bread truck driver is Jesus.

730
00:24:47,567 --> 00:24:49,433
SERVO: And another great thing
about bread,

731
00:24:49,467 --> 00:24:51,367
-it's really great, and...
-CROW: Yeah, that's fine.

732
00:24:51,400 --> 00:24:54,533
MIKE: Thank you, hope you
accept bread into your life.

733
00:24:54,567 --> 00:24:56,633
To sell this piece
of tripe to a magazine?

734
00:24:56,667 --> 00:24:57,967
Jeez.

735
00:24:59,467 --> 00:25:01,667
Grocer goodwill.

736
00:25:01,700 --> 00:25:03,233
Plus volume.

737
00:25:03,267 --> 00:25:05,400
-CROW: Fake acting.
-Selling displays.

738
00:25:05,433 --> 00:25:08,700
MIKE: Flamboyant prancing.
Okay, so you won a bet.

739
00:25:08,733 --> 00:25:12,267
But Bill Dudley's just one guy.

740
00:25:12,300 --> 00:25:15,367
WHITEY: Oh, well,
maybe I better drop down

741
00:25:15,400 --> 00:25:17,867
and see some of the other
fellows on your list.

742
00:25:17,900 --> 00:25:19,500
Oh, no, you don't.

743
00:25:19,533 --> 00:25:21,267
SERVO: And I just waxed that.

744
00:25:21,300 --> 00:25:23,700
After all, that was only a bet.

745
00:25:23,733 --> 00:25:26,933
But from now on, I'm gonna
keep a pretty close watch

746
00:25:26,967 --> 00:25:28,567
on these guys.

747
00:25:28,600 --> 00:25:30,633
CROW: Especially
when they're showering.

748
00:25:30,667 --> 00:25:32,033
[ Music playing ]

749
00:25:32,067 --> 00:25:33,833
And so will I.

750
00:25:33,867 --> 00:25:37,600
[ Singing ]

751
00:25:37,633 --> 00:25:39,500
CROW: Okay, Jerry.
Shut off the projector.

752
00:25:39,533 --> 00:25:40,733
SERVO: Huh.
Some broad, eh, guys?

753
00:25:40,767 --> 00:25:42,167
Just kidding, of course.

754
00:25:42,200 --> 00:25:44,600
Look, you wanna remember
the three basic points,

755
00:25:44,633 --> 00:25:46,133
plus factor...

756
00:25:46,167 --> 00:25:49,167
[ Doors closing ]

757
00:25:59,700 --> 00:26:02,433
You know,
after watching that short,

758
00:26:02,467 --> 00:26:03,900
I've come to understand

759
00:26:03,933 --> 00:26:06,200
that with the limited
shelf space available,

760
00:26:06,233 --> 00:26:08,967
I have to put more stock
in the specialty bread.

761
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:09,767
Yeah, that's right.

762
00:26:09,800 --> 00:26:11,033
You know, you just can't rely

763
00:26:11,067 --> 00:26:12,933
on your sweet doughs anymore.

764
00:26:12,967 --> 00:26:14,600
Nope, it's all going the way

765
00:26:14,633 --> 00:26:16,333
of specialty breads.

766
00:26:16,367 --> 00:26:17,633
Now, in a nutshell,

767
00:26:17,667 --> 00:26:19,267
specialty breads
are all the breads

768
00:26:19,300 --> 00:26:20,800
that aren't white bread.

769
00:26:20,833 --> 00:26:22,300
Yeah, for instance,

770
00:26:22,333 --> 00:26:24,333
